{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
# Carom — Balls, launching, and physics

This file defines how many balls are in play and how each is launched, and how a
ball moves and interacts with the field: the physics loop, collision, and the
paddle bounce and spin mechanic. Every figure below carries the name this
specification gives it.

## The balls

`BALL_COUNT` (`3`) balls are in play at once. Each is the ordinary ball: radius
`BALL_R`, launch speed `SERVE_SPEED`, a `SPEED_MULT` multiplier per paddle hit up
to the `SPEED_CAP` cap, and the spin mechanic below. Each carries its own
velocity and its own spin, and everything in this file applies to every ball
independently.

The balls do not start, reset, or score as a group. When one ball leaves the
field it returns to its own home point and relaunches on its own while the other
two keep playing.
{{else}}
# Carom — Balls, serving, and physics

This file defines how many balls are in play and how they are served, and how a
ball moves and interacts with the field: the physics loop, collision, and the
paddle bounce and spin mechanic. Every figure below carries the name this
specification gives it.

## The ball

One ball is in play: radius `BALL_R`, serve speed `SERVE_SPEED`, a `SPEED_MULT`
multiplier per paddle hit up to the `SPEED_CAP` cap, and the spin mechanic below.
{{/if}}

## Physics

The runtime hands the game the real elapsed time for each frame and imposes no
timestep of its own. Every rate in this specification is integrated against that
delta time: the speeds in units per second, the spin acceleration in units per
second squared, the spin decay per second, and the countdown in seconds.

The outcome of a scenario is independent of how the elapsed time was divided into
frames. The same interval delivered as one long step, as many short ones, or as an
uneven mixture reaches the same state.

The core is render-free: state advances purely from the elapsed time it is handed,
independent of the canvas, of wall-clock time, and of the renderer. Any randomness
runs off the generator seeded from `state.rngState`.

{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
Each frame, for each ball:

1. Apply the spin acceleration (below) to the velocity.
2. Advance position by `velocity * dt`.
3. Resolve collisions with walls, paddles, obstacles, and the other balls.
{{else}}
Each frame, for the ball:

1. Apply the spin acceleration (below) to the velocity.
2. Advance position by `velocity * dt`.
3. Resolve collisions with walls, paddles, and obstacles.
{{/if}}

## Collision

{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
Resolve each ball against the top and bottom walls, the two paddles, the two
obstacles, and the other two balls. Treat each ball as a circle of radius
`BALL_R` and each paddle as an axis-aligned rectangle `PADDLE_W` by `PADDLE_H`.
{{else}}
Resolve the ball against the top and bottom walls, the two paddles, and the two
obstacles. Treat the ball as a circle of radius `BALL_R` and each paddle as an
axis-aligned rectangle `PADDLE_W` by `PADDLE_H`.
{{/if}}

- Top and bottom wall: reflect the vertical velocity (`vy -> -vy`) and push the
  ball back inside the field. Speed is unchanged.
- Obstacle: an obstacle bounce leaves speed unchanged and preserves spin, so the
  spin keeps curving the ball after the bounce.
- Paddle: the next section. A paddle hit is the only collision that changes speed
  and imparts spin.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
- Another ball: the Ball-to-ball collision section below.

Every ball stays inside the field and on the outside of every paddle, obstacle,
and other ball at every speed up to `SPEED_CAP`.
{{else}}

The ball stays inside the field and on the outside of every paddle and obstacle at
every speed up to `SPEED_CAP`.
{{/if}}

## Paddle bounce and spin

{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
When a ball strikes a paddle:
{{else}}
When the ball strikes a paddle:
{{/if}}

1. Reflection angle from contact point. Let
   `offset = (ballCenterY - paddleCenterY) / PADDLE_HALF`, clamped to `[-1, 1]`.
   The outgoing angle from horizontal is `theta = offset * MAX_BOUNCE_ANGLE`
   (`55deg`). Hitting the paddle center sends the ball straight across; hitting
   the top or bottom edge sends it off at `MAX_BOUNCE_ANGLE`.
2. Speed. `speed = min(speed * SPEED_MULT, SPEED_CAP)`. The horizontal direction
   flips to point toward the opposing goal, giving new velocity
   `vx = +/- speed * cos(theta)` (sign toward the opponent) and
   `vy = speed * sin(theta)`.
3. Spin from paddle motion. The paddle's vertical velocity at contact adds spin:
   `spin += paddleVy * SPIN_FROM_PADDLE` (`0.85`), then clamp `spin` to
   `[-SPIN_CLAMP, SPIN_CLAMP]` (`+/-900`). A paddle moving downward as it strikes
   curves the ball one way, a paddle moving upward curves it the other, and a
   stationary paddle leaves the spin as it was.

{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
Spin is a signed scalar carried by each ball. Each frame it applies a lateral
acceleration perpendicular to that ball's direction of travel, of magnitude
`|spin|` in units per second squared, curving the path toward the side the sign of
`spin` selects. Spin decays toward zero exponentially, losing half its magnitude
every `SPIN_HALFLIFE` (`0.8`) seconds (`spin *= 0.5 ^ (dt / SPIN_HALFLIFE)` per
frame). It persists across wall, obstacle, and ball-to-ball bounces, and changes
through paddle hits and this decay alone. Each ball's spin is its own.

## Ball-to-ball collision

The balls collide with each other as elastic circles of equal mass. Two balls are
in contact when the distance between their centers is less than
`BALL_COLLIDE_DIST` (`22`), the sum of their radii. Resolve every pair in contact
each frame, a moving ball against a ball waiting at its home point included.

- Between two moving balls, exchange the velocity components along the line
  joining the two centers, leave the components across that line as they are, and
  push the two balls apart along that line until they no longer overlap.
- A ball waiting to launch is immovable. A moving ball that reaches it reflects
  its own velocity about the contact normal and is pushed clear, while the
  waiting ball keeps both its place and its hold timer.
- A ball-to-ball collision leaves both spins as they are and redistributes the
  velocities the two balls already carry, so a paddle hit remains the only
  collision that changes a ball's speed.

## Home points

Each ball has a fixed home point on the centerline `x = FIELD_CX`, and always
returns to its own. `BALL_HOMES` holds the three, in play order:

- `BALL_HOMES[0]` is `(640, 180)`.
- `BALL_HOMES[1]` is `(640, 360)`, the field center.
- `BALL_HOMES[2]` is `(640, 540)`.

## The hold and the launch

A ball is either waiting at its home point (`held`) or in flight, and each
carries its own hold timer, independent of the other two.

- A waiting ball sits motionless at its home point while its hold timer counts
  down from `HOLD_TIME` (`1.0` second) to `0`. At the start of a match all three
  balls wait at their home points with a full hold timer, so all three launch
  together.
- A waiting ball is solid: it is an immovable round body a moving ball bounces
  off, and it launches when its own hold timer elapses and at no other moment.
- On the first frame a waiting ball's hold timer reaches `0`, that ball launches
  at `SERVE_SPEED` along a uniformly random angle over the full circle, drawn
  from the generator seeded by `state.rngState`. Every launch draws a fresh
  angle, the first launch of a match and every relaunch alike.

## After a point

A point is scored the moment a ball fully passes a goal edge, and only the ball
that crossed is affected: it returns to its own home point, takes a full
`HOLD_TIME` hold timer, and relaunches when that timer elapses. The other two
balls carry on through that respawn, on a field that keeps running. Points from
all three balls accumulate into the same two scores.
{{else}}
Spin is a signed scalar carried by the ball, `state.ball.spin`. Each frame it
applies a lateral acceleration perpendicular to the ball's direction of travel, of
magnitude `|spin|` in units per second squared, curving the path toward the side
the sign of `spin` selects. Spin decays toward zero exponentially, losing half its
magnitude every `SPIN_HALFLIFE` (`0.8`) seconds
(`spin *= 0.5 ^ (dt / SPIN_HALFLIFE)` per frame). It persists across wall and
obstacle bounces, and changes through paddle hits and this decay alone.

## Serving

At the start of the match and after each point, the ball spawns at the field
center `(FIELD_CX, FIELD_CY)` and holds there while `state.holdTimer` counts down
from `HOLD_TIME` (`1.0` second) to `0`. On the first frame the timer reaches `0`,
the ball is served at `SERVE_SPEED`:

- The serve travels toward `state.receiver`, the player who was just scored on.
  The first serve of a match travels toward player one (`"left"`).
- The serve leaves at `SERVE_ANGLE` (`12deg`) from horizontal. Whether that angle
  points up or down the field is yours; either way the serve is within
  `SERVE_MAX_ANGLE` (`30deg`) of horizontal.

An elapsed `holdTimer` is the one thing that launches the ball.

## After a point

When a point is scored, the rally ends. The field holds through the countdown at
the center, then the ball is re-served toward the player who was just scored on.
{{/if}}
